    "description": "Iran’s medical history includes influential Arabic medical writing, the international circulation of physicians’ works, and nineteenth-century encounters with European medicine. Manuscripts and institutional histories reveal different processes of observation, teaching, translation, and reform. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Medical history", "Razi", "Avicenna", "Medical manuscripts", "Qajar Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:24:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:24:44Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "History of Medicine in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Razi, Avicenna, medical manuscripts, Latin translations, and the nineteenth-century introduction of European medicine to Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "books-and-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A history carried by books",
        "content": "Medical history connects Iran with a wider world of Arabic scholarship and Latin translation. Two familiar names illustrate those connections: Muhammad ibn Zakariya al-Razi, known in Europe as Rhazes, and Ibn Sina, known as Avicenna. Their works circulated far beyond the places in which they were written, becoming teaching texts in different linguistic settings. [s1][s2]\n\nA surviving medical book can establish what an author wrote or what a reader copied. It cannot, without additional evidence, tell us how consistently a treatment was used or whether it worked. Keeping that distinction in view allows the intellectual achievement of these writings to be understood without treating their prescriptions as modern clinical guidance.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "razi",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Razi: textbooks and working observations",
        "content": "Razi’s Book of Medicine for Mansur was dedicated to the governor of Rayy, near present-day Tehran. The National Library of Medicine documents its translation into Latin as Liber ad Almansorem and its extensive European readership. A work produced in an Iranian patronage setting thus became part of a much larger medical curriculum. [s1]\n\nHis Comprehensive Book on Medicine, or al-Hawi, had a different origin. It was assembled after his death from working files that combined extracts from earlier authors with his interpretations and clinical cases. This distinction matters: an organized textbook and a physician’s accumulated notes offer different windows onto medical reasoning. [s1]\n\nThe library preserves a portion of al-Hawi copied in 1094. Its survival provides tangible evidence for the transmission of the work, rather than merely a later reputation attached to its author. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "avicenna-and-readers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Avicenna and the changing authority of a classic",
        "content": "Avicenna’s Canon of Medicine, written in Arabic, organized medical knowledge into an influential compendium. The University of Michigan’s exhibition on the history of healing explains that Gerard of Cremona’s Latin translation became an essential university text in Europe from the thirteenth century onward. Editions of works by both Avicenna and Razi continued to appear in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. [s2]\n\nAuthority did not mean universal agreement. Renaissance humanist scholars questioned translations and argued for renewed attention to Greek sources. The Canon’s reception therefore includes criticism as well as admiration. Its historical importance lies partly in the sustained argument it generated about how medical knowledge should be organized and read. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "nineteenth-century-encounters",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Medicine, diplomacy, and reform",
        "content": "In nineteenth-century Iran, European medicine arrived through overlapping channels: court appointments, military missions, consulates, missionary work, and teaching. Shireen Mahdavi’s historical account makes clear that these physicians participated in political relationships as well as medical ones. Access to powerful patients could also create diplomatic influence. [s3]\n\nCrown Prince Abbas Mirza supported medical innovation in Tabriz. The account records vaccination of his household against smallpox and the dispatch of students to England in 1811 to study European medicine. Translation and print helped extend this encounter: a Persian version of William Cormick’s work on smallpox inoculation appeared in 1829–30. [s3]\n\nThese episodes reveal several histories operating together—clinical practice, the education of physicians, patronage, and the circulation of texts. They explain important routes of change without establishing that new methods immediately reached every community or displaced all earlier practice. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
